y that we're talking  about that they thought was really gonna  help another example of the same thing  you are waiting for the bus in the rain  and you talk to the public transit  company and they say that the buses run  every twenty minutes on average so if  you arrive at a random time you would  expect to have to wait about ten minutes  on average that's not true in fact you  end up waiting longer because there's  some variability sometimes the buses are  close together sometimes they're farther  apart if there's a big interval between  buses you are more likely to arrive  during a big interval than a small  interval because it's bigger and it's  this it's this linear waiting again your  probability of falling in any interval  is proportional to the length of that  interval  so I got some data this is Kendall  Square on the red line in Boston and the  MBTA publishes live data that I  downloaded for about a week and  collected the time between trains at  Kendall Square during rush hour and  sometimes they end up really close  together like two minutes and sometimes  they're spread out about 16 minutes  apart this is what it looks like if you  are the MBTA that's the Massachusetts  Bay Transit Authority they run the red  line but if you're a passenger and you  arrive at a random interval this is what  you see and I did this the same way by  weighted resampling of the unbiased  distribution I got the orange biased  distribution so it's shifted off to the  right and sure enough the MBTA will  insist that the average is seven point  eight minutes and passengers will insist  that their average is longer and they  are both correct  they are just averaging over different  things now in this case the difference  is not huge because the variance is not  huge as the variance gets bigger the  effect of this biasing gets much bigger  and there's one extreme example of this  so let me ask you a question are you  popular the answer is no and there's  science there's a reason the answer is  no this is actually from Scientific  American so you know this is true this  is the friendship paradox and that is if  I pick you at random and then I pick one  of your friends at random the chances  are about 80% that your friend has more  friends than you do which seems  paradoxical but think about what a  social network looks like so think of it  as a graph each node is a person each  edge or each line is a friendship  between two people this path by the way  I didn't make this I just found this at  random on somebody's blog so first of  all thank you to them but second of all  weirdly so my name is Allen my wife's  name is Lee  I have no idea who liz is if anybody  asks neither do you so one more time if  you think of this as a graph if you  choose a random node and then choose a  random edge and go to its destination  and then for that node you ask how many  friends it has the probability that you  land on a node is proportional to the  number of edges that lead to that node  which is the number of friends so a node  with degree X or number of friends X is  over sampled by a factor of X now in  this case the distribution of friends is  highly variable  so this is going to make a really big  difference this is a data set I grabbed  this is the stanford data sets for for  studying networks they got some data  from a social network company and i  think like 1998 it just had 4,000 users  at the time but they got the number of  friends for each of them and this is  what that distribution looked like so  there are a few people that have  hundreds of friends and lots of people  that just have a small number of  connections in this social network so  this is highly variable and if I now do  the friend sampling process again I'm  going to overweight popular people  there's what the biased distribution  looks like  so people with a small number of friends  are almost invisible in this sampling  process people with hundreds of friends  show up over and over b